// A pager — the applet `packages/box` has not got, because nothing could ask for a keystroke.
//
// Sixty-four applets and no `more`. `@/packages/tty`'s README says why:
//
// > **What is missing is anything that *selects* one.** `sshd` constructs `Line.create()` and never
// > changes it, the browser terminal likewise, and there is no `stty` for a program to ask with. So
// > an editor still cannot have a keystroke at a time — not because the discipline cannot do it, but
// > **because nothing can say so.**
//
// Measured 2026-09-04: `Line.cbreak()` and `Line.noEcho()` appear **nowhere** outside
// `packages/tty` itself. Two of the three modes were read off a real pty in their own setting, with
// a second oracle written for the purpose — `tools/discipline.py`, because `script -qec cat` cannot
// hold an `stty` — and neither has a caller. **The missing capability shows up as a missing
// program**, which is the clearest evidence for a gap this exercise has found: not an awkward call
// site, an absent one.
//
// This is that program.

import { Result, Bytes } from "core";
import { In, Out, Mode, NotATerminal } from "std";
import { Line, Typed, Effect, Sig } from "@/packages/tty";

/**
 * Page a stream to a terminal, a screenful at a time.
 *
 * `rows` is what a caller read from the environment; asking for it here would make a pager need a
 * `Env`, which is the layering argument `packages/git`'s `statusOf` makes about ignore rules.
 */
export async Result<i32, NotATerminal> more(In in, Out out, i32 rows) {
  // **The whole proposal, in one line.** `setMode` answers the mode it replaced, because restoring
  // is the caller's job and guessing *canonical with echo* is wrong for anyone who was in a
  // password prompt.
  Mode was = try in.setMode(Mode.cbreak());
  defer { _ = in.setMode(was); }

  Line tty = Line.inMode(Mode.cbreak());
  i32 shown = 0;

  try await for (Bytes chunk in in.stream()) {
    // A chunk may hold three keystrokes somebody typed ahead. The discipline is per byte and the
    // generator makes that the loop rather than a carried tail — which is `@/packages/tty`'s own
    // change and the one place here where two rewrites compose without argument.
    for (Typed t in tty.feedAll(chunk)) {
      match (t.effect) {
        Signal(s):    { if (s is Interrupt) { return Ok(130); } }
        Delivered(b): {
          if (b.get(0) == 'q') { return Ok(0); }
          shown = 0;
        }
        else: { }
      }
    }
    if (shown >= rows) { _ = out.write(prompt()); }
  }
  return Ok(0);
}

Bytes prompt() { … }

// ## 1. A mode set on the input changes what correct output looks like
//
// This is the finding, and it is not in `@/packages/tty`'s list.
//
// `Mode.cbreak()` is `ICANON` and `ECHO` off — a property of *reading*. It is also, on every real
// terminal, the moment a program's **writing** has to change: `packages/ssh/src/sshd.wac` says so
// from the other side —
//
// > **The shell's output needs `\r\n`.** A terminal in raw mode does not return the cursor on a bare
// > newline, so output written with `\n` stair-steps down the screen. That translation is `ONLCR`,
// > and it belongs here rather than in the shell, which is writing bytes rather than talking to a
// > terminal.
//
// `sshd` owns both ends of one session, so it can put the translation in the middle. A pager holds
// an `In` and an `Out` that know nothing about each other: `in.setMode(cbreak)` silently makes every
// `out.write` containing a `\n` wrong, and nothing in either signature says a word about it.
//
// So the mode is not a property of the input capability. It is a property of **the terminal**, which
// both capabilities are views onto — the entry `../../../QUESTIONS.md` already has about a mode
// being ambient, arriving from the side that has to *write*. What that entry did not say is that the
// sharing is not merely between two programs: it is between two *capabilities held by one program*,
// which is the case a per-capability grant cannot express at all.
//
// **Half of this moved on 2026-09-04 and the half that moved was not the half above.** `Mode` was
// declared in `@/packages/tty` and is in `std/platform.wac` now, with `In.setMode` beside it — so
// this file compiles as written, where before it imported two names from `"std"` that `"std"` did
// not have. The reason it did not have them is the finding: a `Mode` declared in a *package* is a
// type the capability layer cannot name, so the call could not be declared at all. **Nothing could
// select a mode because the value was one layer too high**, not because nobody wrote the call.
//
// What is still open is exactly the paragraph above: `setMode` is on `In` and the mode is the
// terminal's, so `in.setMode(cbreak)` still silently makes every `out.write` containing a `\n`
// wrong. Moving the type fixed *declarability*. It did not fix *ownership*, and the two were easy to
// mistake for one thing.
//
// ## 2. What could not be written
//
// **`defer { _ = in.setMode(was); }` returns from inside a loop**, which is one of the four
// unsettled parts of what `defer` means. Three of the four exits here are `return`s from inside
// `try await for`, so this file depends on the answer rather than illustrating it — and the failure
// mode if the answer is *no* is a terminal left in cbreak after the pager exits, which is the state
// a person notices by their shell no longer echoing.
//
// **A pager reads and the stream it pages is the same `In`.** `more file` reads the file and the
// keystrokes from different places; `cmd | more` reads the text from standard input and the
// keystrokes from the *terminal*, which is a second input this signature has no room for. The
// shipped `Cli` has one standard input and so does `vision/std`'s `In`, so the pipe case — which is
// the only way anybody uses a pager — needs a capability neither has: **the controlling terminal, as
// distinct from standard input.** That is why this file takes one `In` and pages it, and why that is
// not really a pager.
//
// Worth stating plainly because it is the second time the same shape has appeared: `@/packages/tty`
// wanted a mode on `In` and the mode belongs to the terminal; this wants a *read* from the terminal
// and gets standard input. Both are the same missing distinction, and one program needs both halves.
